 dict(id='S11',citation='Bollerslev, Patton & Quaedvlieg (2016), Exploiting the errors: A simple approach for improved volatility forecasting, Journal of Econometrics192(1),1-18; DOI10.1016/j.jeconom.2015.10.007.',version='49PDF-page2015CREATES working paper rp15_14; publisher identity/DOI verified, not the journal layout.',refs='PDFpp6-7 eqs1-5: IV/RV/IQ/RQ; p11 eq13 basic HARQ; pp11-12 illustrated noisy-day response. p7 visually inspected.',model='HAR with a daily autoregressive coefficient depending on square-root quarticity; fullHARQ lets additional lag coefficients vary.',observation='Finite-sampling RV=IV+eta, variance approximately2IQ/M, with RQ=(M/3)sum r^4. No price-noise-robust variance formula is obtained by merely relabelingRQ.',estimator='Basic level HARQ: RV_t=beta0+(beta1+beta1Q*sqrt(RQ_{t-1}))*RV_{t-1}+beta2*weeklyRV+beta3*monthlyRV+u_t (working-paper eq13).',data='S&P500 and DJIA constituents plus simulations. Exact primary empirical replication not attempted.',finding='Measurement precision changes the predictive weight placed on recentRV; basic HARQ is itself a noise-aware forecasting comparator.',limits='Log-HARQ must follow an explicitly audited specification; replacing labels and retaining the same coefficient formula is not an automatic source-faithful implementation. RQ scale and actual sampling count matter.',replication='Quarticity identities verified numerically. HARQ fitting intentionally held until pilotRQunits/sessionM are resolved.',adds='Use this established benchmark before attributing rough-model gains to a latent mechanism.'),
 dict(id='S12',citation='Patton (2011), Volatility forecast comparison using imperfect volatility proxies, Journal of Econometrics160,246-256; DOI as publisher record in original register.',version='11PDF-page published article from author Duke site.',refs='PDFp3 Definition1 and losses5-13; p4 exact normal/t calculations; p5 /journal250 Table1; Table1 visually inspected.',model='Forecasts of a latent conditional variance evaluated by imperfect conditionally unbiased proxies; separates desired functional from arbitrary losses.',observation='Squared return, realized variance and range proxies with distribution-specific properties; robustness means preservation of expected forecast ranking under specified conditional mean assumptions.',estimator='Analytical expected-loss minimizers. QLIKE is log(f)+proxy/f, MSE is also robust under the stated assumptions. Losses on transformed targets need not be.',data='Our selected Table1 is theoretical, standardized sigma^2=1 with t6,t10 and Gaussian returns; no market data needed.',finding='Noisy-proxy loss choice can change optimal forecasts even when variance is fixed. This directly affects interpretation of an economic comparison.',limits='Conditional unbiasedness is essential. A robust ranking does not prove structural identification, and a proxy-based utility criterion targets a different objective.',replication='Prespecified Table1 replication attempted across27cells at tolerance0.015:25passed. MAE-prop t6 exact numerical optimum2.821602 versus printed2.73; t10 exact2.580113 versus2.55. Source footnote explicitly calls this row a numerical approximation. Preserve discrepancies.',adds='A reproducible foundational check of our evaluation conventions; not a roughness-estimator replication.'),
